{
  "title": "Validate concurrent reads without equating live diagnostics",
  "body": "#### Summary\n\n##### Problem\n\n`TC_RT_1_2` treats SDK cache metadata, fabric-specific data and changing diagnostics as identical across independent reads, producing false consistency failures.\n\n##### Solution\n\n- Exclude SDK cache metadata, not cluster attributes.\n- Use compressed fabric identity and validate fabric-filtered lists and `CurrentFabricIndex` without requiring cross-fabric equality; keep same-fabric and shared-value checks strict.\n- Bound clock, countdown and uptime differences by the measured read window; validate known live diagnostics while retaining strict error-counter checks.\n- Compare Thread-table membership and stable topology independently of entry order, preserving duplicate counts and rejecting malformed entries.\n\n#### Testing\n\n- 75 read-consistency unit tests, including permutations, membership/topology changes, malformed responses, fabric boundaries and timing limits.\n- Preserved-response replay plus synthetic permutation controls; physical multi-entry churn was not exercised.\n- Ten passing combined Ubuntu/Nordic `TC_RT_1_2` iterations. The table-order correction is covered by CPU tests and replay, not that hardware run.\n- Local Ruff, autopep8 and isort checks pass.\n",
  "draft": false,
